  "text": "Due to various biases of retrospective study, there were differences in the 2y-OS between the two groups of patients in the subgroup analysis of Non-MPR and Non-pCR, though there is no significant difference in DFS. One plausible explanation is that NCIT itself plays a predominant role in reducing postoperative recurrence and metastasis, particularly distant metastasis, whereas adjuvant immunotherapy may confer only a limited benefit[15]. Moreover, whether this translates into a durable overall survival benefit will require longer follow-up. In the present study, pathological response emerged as a key factor for stratifying surgical patients for adjuvant immunotherapy. The significant OS benefit observed in Non-MPR/Non-pCR patients therefore supports selective escalation of systemic therapy in these high-risk cases. These findings are consistent with the JCOG1109 trial, in which intensified triplet neoadjuvant chemotherapy (docetaxel/cisplatin/5-fluorouracil) significantly improved OS without increasing surgical morbidity[27]. Conversely, the absence of any survival improvement in MPR/pCR patients suggests that routine adjuvant immunotherapy may represent overtreatment in this low-risk subgroup. Future strategies should integrate pathological response assessment with perioperative biomarkers (e.g., ctDNA dynamics) to guide the precision delivery of adjuvant immunotherapy[28]."
